2026-10-22 11:59:51,568 | INFO | Knowledge Base ingestion response: {'status': 'success', 'message': 'All documents got in successfully.', 'documents_processed': 2, 'documents_failed': 0, 'chunks_inserted': 995, 'documents': [{'title': 'Human Geography', 'document_type': 'Book', 'document_id': '9dd0be90-fb29-4ebd-8ed7-416c368a33d5', 'tag': 'general', 'summary': 'This document discusses the nature and scope of human geography, emphasizing its integrative, empirical, and practical approach. It covers the relationship between the physical and human worlds, the spatial distributions of human phenomena, and the social and economic differences between different parts of the world.', 'source': 'Geography_compressed.pdf'}, {'title': 'The Harappan Civilisation', 'document_type': 'General', 'document_id': '8beb3510-f209-49eb-973d-7fbde61a1968', 'tag': 'general', 'summary': 'The Harappan Civilisation is also known as the Indus Valley Civilisation. The term Harappa is derived from the place where the civilization was identified. The total time span of the civilization ranges from 6000 BCE to 1300 BCE. The early phase, termed as Early Harappan (6000 BCE-2600 BCE) is a formative phase of the civilization. The urban phase of the civilization, termed as Mature Harappan (2600 BCE-1900 BCE) is the most prosperous phase. The decline of the civilization starts around 1900 BCE. The distinctive Harappan pottery, bricks, seals, weights, beads, copper and bronze articles, belonging to the Harappan civilization have been found in the region, as apart as Afghanistan, Baluchistan, Sind and Punjab provinces of Pakistan and the Indian States of Jammu and Kashmir, Himachal Pradesh, Punjab, Haryana, Rajasthan, Uttar Pradesh, Gujarat and Maharashtra.', 'source': 'History_compressed.pdf'}], 'errors': []}
